 :adr/authority "owner instruction, 2026-09-09"
 :adr/repos "kotoba-lang/amu, kotoba-lang/pattern, kotoba-lang/kiyaku"
 :adr/related "adr-2609092700-no-tree-no-ceiling-and-the-first-consumer, adr-2609092600-a-program-outgrew-the-document-and-the-corpus-hid-a-bug, kotoba-lang/amu#912, kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema#78"
 :adr/body "Continuing 2026-09-09. The pattern library was finished and had a first
consumer, but that consumer could not REQUIRE it -- so this records the
blocker, the rule that removed it, and the composition it bought.

## What was blocked

A module that declared `:schemas` could not be required by another module.
The project linker refused with \"only one :export, one :capabilities and
alias-only :require clauses are admitted\", and the message named the
REQUIRING module's ns form, which reads as a typo in the consumer and is not
one.

It matters because records are how a Kotoba function carries more than five
arguments (`:max-parameters 5`), so a library that does real work declares
`:schemas`. `kotoba-lang/pattern`'s matcher and compiler are records
throughout. Nothing could require them: a consumer compiled them separately
and put the artifacts together in its own host. That works, and it is not
composition -- the question the consumer actually asks lived in no artifact.

## The rule

Each module is analysed on its own, so a schema name resolves inside the
module that declares it and the tables never have to merge for typing. What
CAN go wrong is two modules declaring the SAME NAME with DIFFERENT definitions
and passing a value of it across the import boundary: both sides type-check
locally, and the linked call means something neither module wrote.

So: identical definitions are admitted -- that is what a shared schema looks
like when two modules spell it the same way -- and a differing pair is refused
by name, with both modules in the ex-data. The linked namespace then carries
the union, because it is compiled as ONE module and its emitted signatures
reference schemas by name.

The old docstring said this collision rule was the missing piece, and it was
right about that. It was not the only missing piece.

## Three causes, and only one was readable

1. The clause allowlist in `module-info`.
2. No collision rule, and no merged table in the linked namespace -- the
   frontend refused \"value type references a schema outside the closed
   namespace table\".
3. `source-text` re-serialises every module and reads it back, and a map whose
   keys share a namespace prints as `#:sl{:r [...]}`. The Kotoba reader has no
   `#:` dispatch and answers \"unsupported reader dispatch\", so the round trip
   failed and was attributed to the module as a READ error. This was latent
   for any project module holding such a map, not only for schemas.

Causes 1 and 2 were both fixed while the reproducer still failed. Cause 3 was
found by re-running the reproducer, not by reading -- which is the general
shape: the fix that reading produces is the fix for the cause reading can see.

## Evidence

kotoba-lang/amu#912. Suite 1359 tests / 9837 assertions, 0 failures. Each half
was broken in turn and the right check went red on its own: removing the
collision check reddened only the collision test; dropping the merged clause
reddened the merged-table test at the frontend refusal; restoring namespaced-
map printing reddened both schema tests at the read failure.

Four CLI reproducers, before -> after: a project requiring a `:schemas`
dependency, exit 65 -> ok; two modules spelling one schema identically, exit
65 -> ok; two spelling it differently, exit 65 for the wrong reason -> exit 65
naming the collision; a module requiring `pattern-vm`, exit 65 -> ok.

kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema#78 was filed against the wrong repo (the grammar
already admitted `:schemas`; the project linker did not) and is closed
pointing here.

## What it bought

`kiyaku`'s `kotoba/postal_core.kotoba` now carries the first
`(:require [pattern-vm :as vm])` in the workspace and owns `valid-postal?`
end to end, including the unknown-country fallback that had been left with the
caller for the same reason everything else was. The parity test compiles ONE
artifact and asks that one function: 42 checks, up from 35.

Two things the rewire measured rather than reasoned:

* `blank?` walking by `+ 1` is wrong off ASCII. `string-code-point-at` indexes
  BYTES, so the walk lands mid-character and traps with
  `string-code-point-boundary`. The ASCII corpus never reached it; widening
  `blank?` by one code point reached it immediately. This is the corpus-hides-
  a-bug shape from adr-2609092600 again, in a different module, found the same
  way -- by trying to break the check rather than by reading it.
* The remaining gap is real and pinned rather than described: `str/blank?` is
  `Character/isWhitespace`, the module is ASCII. The parity test asserts the
  gap in the direction it exists, so widening the module fails that probe on
  the day it happens rather than the day someone remembers.

## What this does not say

It does not say every library should declare `:schemas`. It says a library
that needs records is no longer excluded from being a dependency, which was
never a decision anyone made -- it was a clause allowlist, a missing rule, and
a printer."}]
